2. Why separate if statements can produce multiple results
Each independent if statement is checked separately. If conditions overlap, more than one block can run and print multiple
decisions. An if–elif–else chain stops after the first matching branch, so it produces exactly one decision. Conditions
should be ordered from the most specific/highest boundary logic to the remaining cases.
3. Two implementations and comparison
def separate_if(units, ration_card, age_over_80):
    results = []
    if units <= 100:
        results.append("Full subsidy")
    if 101 <= units <= 200 and ration_card:
        results.append("Partial subsidy")
    if units > 200 and not age_over_80:
        results.append("No subsidy")
    if units > 200 and age_over_80:
        results.append("Special subsidy")
    if 101 <= units <= 200 and not ration_card:
        results.append("No subsidy")
    return results
def chained(units, ration_card, age_over_80):
    if units <= 100:
        return "Full subsidy"
    elif 101 <= units <= 200:
        if ration_card:
            return "Partial subsidy"
        else:
            return "No subsidy"
    else:
        if age_over_80:
            return "Special subsidy"
        else:
            return "No subsidy"
tests = [
    (100, False, False), (101, True, False),
    (101, False, False), (200, True, False),
    (200, False, False), (201, False, False),
    (250, False, True), (80, True, False)
]
for case in tests:
    print(case, "->", separate_if(*case), "|", chained(*case))
The chained version is preferable because each input maps to one clear outcome. The separate-if version is useful to
demonstrate the bug risk, but its output is a list because multiple branches can run.
