3. String concepts
Strings are immutable: once a string object is created, its characters cannot be changed in place. Operations such as
replace() and upper() return new strings. Therefore text.upper() does not modify text unless the result is assigned, for
example text = text.upper().
For the 9-character form, s[-4:] returns the final four digits and s[4:-4] returns the one-letter series. For the 10-character
form, s[-4:] still returns the final four digits and s[4:-4] returns the two-letter series.
4. Complete-the-code answer and alternative
The normalise() and is_valid() functions above complete TODO 1 and TODO 2 using only string operations and slicing. A
regular-expression alternative is:
import re
def is_valid_regex(reg):
    return re.fullmatch(r"[A-Z]{2}[0-9]{2}[A-Z]{1,2}[0-9]{4}", reg) is not None
Both approaches take O(L) time for a string of length L. Slicing and string methods make each field check explicit and are
easy for beginners to trace. A regular expression is compact and makes the complete format visible in one pattern, but can
be harder to maintain for someone unfamiliar with regex syntax.
