3. Local, global and nonlocal scope
count = 10                 # global name
def show_local():
    count = 2              # local to this function
    return count
def change_global():
    global count
    count = 20
def make_counter():
    value = 0              # enclosing (nonlocal) scope
    def increment():
        nonlocal value
        value += 1
        return value
    return increment
print(show_local())       # 2
change_global()
print(count)              # 20
counter = make_counter()
print(counter(), counter())  # 1 2
A local name exists within its function. A global name is defined at module level. A nonlocal name refers to a variable in the
nearest enclosing function scope. The global keyword is required when assigning to a global name inside a function;
without it, Python treats the assigned name as local. Avoid changing globals where possible because it creates hidden
dependencies; parameters and return values are easier to test.
