4. n = 1,000 and deployment decision
The memoised recursive version may raise RecursionError at n = 1,000 because Python has a finite recursion limit
(inspect it with sys.getrecursionlimit()). The number of cached states is linear, but the call depth is also approximately n.
Raising the recursion limit can risk exhausting the interpreter stack and is not the best deployment choice.
The iterative version uses O(n) time and O(1) extra space and avoids recursion depth limits. For a requirement of up to
1,000 steps, I would deploy the loop version. The recursive and memoised versions are useful for explaining the
recurrence and repeated work; the loop is more robust for the stated input size.
import sys
print("recursion limit:", sys.getrecursionlimit())
print(ways_loop(1000))
# ways_memo(1000) may raise RecursionError on the default limit.
5. Complexity comparison
Naive recursion: O(fn) time and O(n) stack. Memoised recursion: O(n) time and O(n) cache/stack. Iterative two-variable
method: O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space.
